 "deepDive": {
  "domain": "Technology, AI & cyber",
  "title": "Federal breaches pile up in Washington; India defines sovereign AI",
  "body": "**The US government had another bad cyber week.** Coming soon after the NSANet outage, the FBI NCIC breach and a USDA intrusion, two more incidents came out on Nov 24–25. First, an unclassified CIA/ODNI system for open-source analysis was breached. CNN and the NYT report that early signs point to China and possibly an AI-assisted hacking tool (Reported). Officials say classified networks were not touched. Second, Treasury's FinCEN took systems offline after someone got into part of its network (Reported).\n\nThe White House answered with a critical-infrastructure package (Confirmed). Agencies must patch known-exploited flaws within three days, CISA gets about 400 more staff, and the FCC will move against risky foreign-made routers. Whether this changes anything depends on funding. Congress has not yet passed a budget for the year (the stopgap deadline is Dec 11), and CISA has lost staff over the past two years.\n\n**India is working out what 'sovereign AI' means.** MeitY Secretary S. Krishnan said Sarvam AI, a flagship grantee of the IndiaAI Mission, did not break mission rules by offering access to Chinese open-weight models such as DeepSeek on its platform. He also said India's long-promised AI regulation consultation papers are in their final stages. Things to watch in them: liability, content labelling, and whether sovereignty conditions on mission grantees get tighter.\n\nPoland and Kraków had no major new cyber incidents. The Autopay breach still has no published count of affected customers.",
